gin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-4=1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,1=1\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 2 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-1=2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,4=2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nCD=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n" "Here, $C$ is a $2 \\times 3$ matrix and $D$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $C$ and $3$ rows in $D,$ so $C$ and $D$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}2}} \\times \\color{#2E8B57}{\\fbox{3}}}{C} \\underset{\\color{#2E8B57}\\checkmark}{\\cdot} \\underset{{\\color{#2E8B57}\\fbox{3}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}2}}}{D} = \n\t\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}2}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}2}}}{CD}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $CD,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n-4=1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-3)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n1=1\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 2\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n-1=2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-3)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n4=2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nCD=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186100741 :question/problem "Compute the product \n $\\begin{bmatrix}\n       -1  \\\\\n \t0  \\\\\n        -2\n \t\n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        2 & -5\n    \\end{bmatrix}.$" "Compute the product\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix} -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & -5 \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186100741 :question/worked-solution "Here, the first factor is a $3 \\times 1$ matrix and the second factor is a $1 \\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in the first matrix and $1$ row in the second matrix, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times {\\color{green}\\fbox{1}} {\\color{green}\\checkmark} {\\color{green}\\fbox{1}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} = \n\t\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n       -1  \\\\\n \t0  \\\\\n        -2\n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        2 & -5\n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n\t&= \n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        (-1)\\cdot 2 & (-1)\\cdot (-5)  \\\\\n        0\\cdot 2 & 0\\cdot (-5) \\\\\n        (-2)\\cdot 2 & (-2)\\cdot (-5)\n    \\end{bmatrix} \\\\[5pt]\n\n\t&=\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n       -2 & 5  \\\\\n       0 &  0  \\\\\n       -4 &  10 \n    \\end{bmatrix} \n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, the 